# Post-fork copyright attribution

All code **written after the fork** (new files, and new content in this fork) is attributed to
**QuickFAST contributors** — never to Object Computing, Inc. (OCI). OCI holds copyright only on
the pre-fork upstream code it actually wrote.

## Required header for every new file

C++ sources and headers (`src/**/*.cpp`, `src/**/*.h`):

```cpp
// Copyright (c) 2026, QuickFAST contributors.
// All rights reserved.
// See the file license.txt for licensing information.
```

Use the comment syntax of the file's language (`#` for CMake/Python/shell, `<!-- -->` for HTML),
but keep the three lines and the exact wording, including the comma and trailing period.

## Hard rules

- Do **not** write `Copyright (c) 2026, Object Computing, Inc.` or any other year paired with OCI on
  a file created after the fork.
- Do **not** ship a bare `// Copyright (c) 2026` with no holder.
- Do **not** edit or remove existing pre-fork OCI headers (e.g. `Copyright (c) 2009, Object
  Computing, Inc.`) on files that came from upstream — leave them intact even when modifying the file.
- When you notice a post-fork file carrying an OCI header, fix it to the contributors line.

```cpp
// ❌ new file, wrong holder
// Copyright (c) 2026 Object Computing, Inc.

// ❌ no holder
// Copyright (c) 2026

// ✅
// Copyright (c) 2026, QuickFAST contributors.
```
